00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:38.320
on april 30 1789 george washington took the oath&nbsp;
of office as the first president of the united&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:43.360
states having been universally elected by the&nbsp;
electoral college the constitution did not specify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.560
how the executive branch of government of which&nbsp;
the revolutionary war hero was now the head was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:52.640
to conduct its business leaving washington&nbsp;
to construct the apparatus of governments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:56.880
around him he decided to ask congress to&nbsp;
create three new government departments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.520
state war and treasury the secretaries of&nbsp;
these departments appointed by the president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.560
and confirmed by the senate would together with&nbsp;
the attorney general formed the cabinet a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:11.280
of men who would oversee the everyday business&nbsp;
of executive government and advise the president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:16.480
on policy matters almost 250 years later this&nbsp;
is still how the executive branch functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:22.240
though today's cabinet is made up of 25 members&nbsp;
instead of four washington's trusted wartime aide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:26.720
decarb alexander hamilton was selected by the&nbsp;
president to serve as the first secretary of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:31.600
treasury the department that was to oversee the&nbsp;
fledgling country's fragile economy confirmed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.840
the senate in september hamilton immediately got&nbsp;
to work with his usual gusto the first problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:41.360
was the biggest the government had no money to pay&nbsp;
for its business the entire country suffered from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:46.240
a lack of ready cash and the obvious solution was&nbsp;
to borrow money but with no established credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:50.480
rating none of the large european banks were&nbsp;
willing to give the united states government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:56.080
a loan to resolve this hamilton proposed to create&nbsp;
a public debt using debts incurred by the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:00.160
states during the revolutionary war which the&nbsp;
federal government would assume if he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.880
show the u.s could pay back its internal debt&nbsp;
then foreign banks would feel confident that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:10.640
could pay back any international debts incurred in&nbsp;
addition the bonds could be traded by speculators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:16.160
known as stock jabbers as currency which would&nbsp;
stimulate the american economy hamilton ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:20.720
into unexpectedly fierce opposition from a group&nbsp;
of political figures headed by the newly appointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:25.200
secretary of state thomas jefferson jefferson&nbsp;
had been involved with the american experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.160
from the beginning he was the principal author&nbsp;
of the declaration of independence but jefferson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:34.960
was skeptical of the constitution and&nbsp;
mistrusted a strong federal government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:39.760
taking on a public debt increased the power of&nbsp;
the federal government and opened the door to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:45.440
possible corruption which jefferson associated&nbsp;
with tyrannical rule president washington wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:49.600
both men in his cabinet as he believed both would&nbsp;
offer valuable service to the new government he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:55.360
encouraged them to find a compromise and there was&nbsp;
something jefferson's faction wanted the federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.360 --> 00:13:00.160
capital it was currently located in new york city&nbsp;
but southern politicians like jefferson wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:05.040
it moved further south closer to their states a&nbsp;
deal was struck the capital would be moved first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:09.840
to philadelphia and then eventually to a planned&nbsp;
federal site on the potomac river that formed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:15.360
border between maryland and virginia in exchange&nbsp;
the revolutionary war dead would be assumed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:20.000
federal government hamilton was a new yorker at&nbsp;
heart and he knew losing the capital would hurt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.200
his home city but he also knew it was what was&nbsp;
best for the country as a whole so he agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:33.840
and so it was that washington dc was born and&nbsp;
the foundation of the american economy was laid

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:39.920
the deal between hamilton and jefferson over the&nbsp;
capital was the last time the two founding fathers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.760
were able to work together on anything the two men&nbsp;
were fairly similar to each other and in another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:48.000
life they could have probably been close friends&nbsp;
but both men were stubbornly committed to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:53.600
principles believing the future of the nation&nbsp;
was at stake and they both had wildly different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:58.880
ideas about what direction the united states&nbsp;
should take jefferson a plantation owner who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:04.320
owned hundreds of slaves believed that a largely&nbsp;
agrarian nation populated by small-holding farmers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.560
was the most democratic form for an economy&nbsp;
to take in foreign policy he favored france&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:12.640
believing the upheavals of the french revolution&nbsp;
were a continuation of the american struggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:16.640
to overthrow monarchy most importantly he&nbsp;
favored a weak federal government that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:20.960
subordinate to the whims of state government&nbsp;
particularly large ones like virginia he and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:25.360
his supporters like hamilton's collaborator&nbsp;
on the federalist papers james madison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:29.520
formed a faction they called democratic&nbsp;
republicans today historians refer to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:34.240
as the jeffersonian republicans to differentiate&nbsp;
them from the modern democratic and republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:39.360
parties alexander hamilton by contrast believed&nbsp;
the future of america lay not in farming but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:44.080
commerce through manufacturing and trade because&nbsp;
90 percent of american trade at the time came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:48.560
through great britain hamilton favored them&nbsp;
not because he wanted to restore british rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:52.720
in america but because he believed it benefited&nbsp;
his nation economically and he believed that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:57.520
best way to govern the country was through a&nbsp;
strong federal government with broad authority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:02.400
he and his faction were called federalists george&nbsp;
washington was officially neutral not belonging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:07.520
to either of the two factions in fact he cautioned&nbsp;
against forming political parties at all believing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:11.760
they would lead to the downfall of the republic&nbsp;
but he was unable to keep members of the cabinet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:16.080
from gearing up for an all-out assault on each&nbsp;
other their weapon of choice was the country's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:20.640
newspapers newspapers of the period contained very&nbsp;
little objective reporting instead they were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:25.680
vehicle for partisan writers to exhort their cause&nbsp;
and to attack their opponents through a series of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:30.720
long opinion pieces most of these were written&nbsp;
under pen names but everybody knew who the authors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:35.440
were on the federalist side this was frequently&nbsp;
alexander hamilton where jefferson preferred to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:39.840
avoid direct confrontation and used proxies&nbsp;
all the while hamilton continued to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.640
moulding the american economy into his vision&nbsp;
he founded a national bank established a u.s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:49.840
mint in philadelphia to produce currency formed&nbsp;
the u.s coast guard to prevent smuggling a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:54.000
problem when most of the government's money&nbsp;
came from customs duties and worked to expand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.800
domestic manufacturing and to end the country's&nbsp;
over alliance and foreign imports washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:04.080
generally sided with hamilton in disputes between&nbsp;
his treasury and state secretary something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:09.760
incensed jefferson to the point that he resigned&nbsp;
as post at the end of 1793. by now jefferson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:15.440
was convinced that not only was hamilton taking&nbsp;
the country down the wrong path but that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:21.440
actively corrupt probably enriching himself either&nbsp;
through stock jobbing or through secret deals with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:26.320
the british the attacks in hamilton's policies&nbsp;
turned increasingly personal portraying him as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:30.720
evil shadow lurking behind the chair of&nbsp;
the noble washington some of the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:36.480
vicious attacks criticized his foreign origins&nbsp;
and even his illegitimacy all of this weighed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:41.520
heavily on hamilton he felt underappreciated he&nbsp;
was also heavily in debt and able to support his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:47.040
family on his government salary certainly there&nbsp;
was no hidden fortune derived from corruption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:52.880
finally on january 31 1795 hamilton resigned&nbsp;
as treasury secretary returning to private life

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:03.040
in 1796 george washington decided not to&nbsp;
run for a third term as president wanting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:07.760
avoid the perception that it was a post that&nbsp;
one person should hold for life washington's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:12.320
magnaminity set up the first competitive election&nbsp;
in the country's history the logical choice for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:16.320
hamilton and the federalists was to support&nbsp;
the vice president john adams believing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:20.400
to be the only person who could defeat thomas&nbsp;
jefferson something that was proved out when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:25.200
adams was elected president but john adams was&nbsp;
no george washington adams has spent decades in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:29.760
public service having collaborated with jefferson&nbsp;
on the declaration of independence and serving as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:34.320
foreign ambassador to the courts of europe but&nbsp;
in all that time adams had never developed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:39.840
thick skin needed to work in politics he took even&nbsp;
the smallest slide personally and held grudges for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:44.720
a long time he spent months at a time outside of&nbsp;
the capital in his home of massachusetts dictating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:49.200
his orders via letters and most importantly&nbsp;
for hamilton adams wanted nothing to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:54.880
him scorning his advice hamilton chafed at the&nbsp;
loss of influence and looked for a way to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.880 --> 00:18:00.160
back into the action revolutionary france provided&nbsp;
him with that opportunity the united states had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:06.080
concluded a trade deal with great britain a few&nbsp;
years earlier the controversial j treaty the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:10.400
french who were at war with britain viewed this as&nbsp;
a betrayal and a series of diplomatic skirmishes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:14.640
threatened to descend into war president adams&nbsp;
felt he had no choice but to raise an army to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:18.960
defend the country in the face of a possible&nbsp;
french invasion a natural choice to command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:23.120
this army was george washington but washington&nbsp;
would only accept the command if his second in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:29.200
command was alexander hamilton so hamilton was&nbsp;
appointed a major general in 1798 with washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:33.920
ailing at his home in mount vernon hamilton&nbsp;
effectively ran the army taking charge of training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:40.160
organization and supplies adams didn't want war&nbsp;
with france or anyone else pinning his hopes on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:44.960
diplomatic solution this brought him into conflict&nbsp;
with hamilton who still clung to boyish notions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:50.000
of military glory everything started to go wrong&nbsp;
for hamilton at once his great friend and patron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:55.440
george washington died in 1799. adams stripped&nbsp;
funding for the army privately worrying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.440 --> 00:19:00.560
hamilton wanted to march on the capitol and set&nbsp;
himself up as a dictator america's first political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:05.280
sex scandal came to light as newspaper reports&nbsp;
revealed the spicy details of an affair hamilton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:11.200
had in 1792 with a woman named maria reynolds who&nbsp;
it appeared was extorting him for money to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:15.920
the affair a secret hamilton in a bizarre display&nbsp;
decided to admit to the affair in a published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:20.560
pamphlet that was widely circulated hamilton&nbsp;
wanted to defend himself from corruption charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:25.600
but the pamphlet just made things worse things&nbsp;
came to a head in 1800 when hamilton furious at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:30.240
john adams for dismantling his army after a peace&nbsp;
deal with napoleon was reached turned his pen on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:35.360
the president attacking his fitness for office&nbsp;
and endorsing a south carolina federalist charles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:40.880
pickney instead many people privately agreed with&nbsp;
hamilton about adams character but the attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:46.160
on adam split the federalist vote ensuring the&nbsp;
election of hamilton's greatest rival jefferson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:50.480
to the highest office adams never forgave&nbsp;
hamilton and spent the rest of his life attacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:54.720
hamilton's character in print as for hamilton&nbsp;
the political blunders seemed to have destroyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:59.360
his own career which probably prevented him from&nbsp;
having any chance of running for president himself

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:10.640
in 1804 vice president aaron burr was a man with&nbsp;
no friends in the election of 1800 he had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:15.200
the jeffersonian candidate for vice president by&nbsp;
the way the constitution was originally written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:19.920
whoever got the most electoral votes became&nbsp;
president and the runner-up became vice president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:25.680
the problem with this came when the top two&nbsp;
candidates in this case jefferson and burr tied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:29.760
in the electoral vote throwing the election to&nbsp;
the house of representatives burr was a political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:35.280
opportunist and used the tie votes to try and gain&nbsp;
the office for himself at the expense of jefferson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:40.720
courting disgruntled federalists to try and vote&nbsp;
for him instead this didn't work and the newly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:44.880
elected vice president found himself shut out of&nbsp;
jefferson's government everyone knew that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:50.240
jefferson came up for re-election in 1804 burr was&nbsp;
going to be dropped from the ticket burr decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:54.240
that his next move was to run for governor of his&nbsp;
home state of new york hoping to build a following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:59.440
there for a possible return to washington but what&nbsp;
was left of alexander hamilton's power base was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:04.640
new york and if there was one man hamilton didn't&nbsp;
want him power it was aaron burr hamilton believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:09.680
that burr only cared about himself and that he&nbsp;
had no principles except whatever would ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:14.160
his own advancement he disliked burst so much that&nbsp;
he campaigned in the house for jefferson and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:20.800
1800 thai election a strange irony that was not&nbsp;
lost to hamilton in 1804 hamilton campaigned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:26.080
hard for burr's opponent morgan lewis playing a&nbsp;
key role in his victory in that race burr hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:30.640
forgotten hamilton's politicking in 1800 and felt&nbsp;
that this was the second time the former secretary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:35.520
of the treasury had derailed his political&nbsp;
ambitions he challenged hamilton to a pistol duel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:40.640
duels or affairs of honor were holdovers from&nbsp;
the days of chivalry a form of ritualized murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:45.280
in which the two combatants stood 20 paces apart&nbsp;
and shot at each other people dying in duels was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:50.800
relatively rare it was more about demonstrating&nbsp;
their bravery and being willing to face down death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:56.160
in the name of honor in that way it was more a&nbsp;
show of political grandstanding than anything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.160 --> 00:22:01.200
burr wanted to try and use a showdown with&nbsp;
hamilton to resurrect his failing political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:06.000
career and hamilton never wanted to back down&nbsp;
from a challenge happily obliged him hamilton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:09.600
let it be known in the days preceding the duel&nbsp;
that he had no intention of trying to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:15.280
burr he intended to throw away his shot by firing&nbsp;
his gun in the air a demonstration that he held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:20.560
no ill-will towards his opponent many such duels&nbsp;
ended this way with both combatants walking away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:26.000
unscathed their personal honor intact dueling&nbsp;
was illegal in new york so the two men met across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:32.240
the river in new jersey on the morning of july the&nbsp;
11th 1804. in an ironic twist of fate it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:36.720
same spot where two years earlier hamilton's&nbsp;
eldest son philip had been killed in a duel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:42.480
himself hamilton probably fired first and his shot&nbsp;
missed probably on purpose burst shot did not miss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:46.960
the bullet struck hamilton in the abdomen&nbsp;
doing major damage to his internal organs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:51.520
lodging in his spine hamilton knew immediately&nbsp;
that he'd been mortally wounded he was taken back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:56.000
to his home in new york where he lasted until&nbsp;
the next day saying goodbye to his distraught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:01.840
wife and children as well as his friends he was&nbsp;
only 49 when the bullet finally claimed his life

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:12.000
the death of alexander hamilton proved to be the&nbsp;
death now for the federalist party that he led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:16.880
jeppesen's party now held unchallenged ascendancy&nbsp;
as jeffersonian republicans held the presidency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:22.800
from 1800 to 1824 but hamilton's financial system&nbsp;
which jefferson hated so much survived because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:27.600
as jefferson's treasury secretary albert&nbsp;
gallatin pointed out the system was so perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:32.320
constructed any attempt to dismantle it would harm&nbsp;
the country in fact when president james madison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:37.600
allowed the first bank of the united states to&nbsp;
expire during his administration it precipitated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:42.240
a financial crisis that forced him to sheepishly&nbsp;
found the second bank of the united states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:47.360
to restore american finance hamilton's death&nbsp;
also killed the political career of aaron burr&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:52.160
though he escaped murder charges filed against&nbsp;
him he never held another office after his term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:57.520
as vice president ended in 1805. he spent the rest&nbsp;
of his life moving from place to place as a pariah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.520 --> 00:24:02.240
never showing remorse for killing hamilton&nbsp;
hamilton meanwhile was widely mourned especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:06.720
in new york city his funeral on july the 14th&nbsp;
was the largest in the city's long history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:11.840
every business in new york was closed that day and&nbsp;
so many people marched in the funeral procession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:17.120
that it took hours to complete not everyone was&nbsp;
sorry to see hamilton die though his two longtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:22.160
political antagonists john adams and thomas&nbsp;
jefferson lived two decades after hamilton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:27.280
death and their long-standing grievances against&nbsp;
the man made their way into their writings that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:31.680
were published for posterity hamilton wasn't&nbsp;
around to defend himself or write his memoirs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:36.080
explaining what he did and why which surely&nbsp;
would have been a masterpiece of literature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:40.400
it has only been recently that hamilton's&nbsp;
reputation has undergone a re-evaluation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:44.720
most particularly through a widely successful&nbsp;
broadway musical based on hamilton's life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:49.280
it's remarkable how accurate alexander hamilton's&nbsp;
predictions about the american economy were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:54.320
hamilton believed that commerce was the key to the&nbsp;
new country's future he predicted the industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:59.360
revolution the rise of manufacturing to replace&nbsp;
farming the primacy of cities over rural areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:03.760
the power of stock trading and banking and the&nbsp;
national debt in short it's everything that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:08.320
modern capitalism function equally important&nbsp;
every president after george washington has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:13.280
built on the power of the executive branch first&nbsp;
proposed by alexander hamilton the united states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:18.240
has a robust federal government that keeps the&nbsp;
many states bound together precisely because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:22.720
men like hamilton believed that this was the&nbsp;
only way to keep the country from inevitably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:27.760
splitting itself apart whether you think the&nbsp;
legacy he left makes him a hero or a villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:33.120
you have to admit that few of the founding fathers&nbsp;
achieved more than he did few other americans have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:38.960
ever started as lowly as he did and risen as&nbsp;
high and the tragedy of his life is that we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:44.400
never know what else he might have been able to&nbsp;
achieve had he not been cut down in his prime

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:52.720
so i really hope you found this video&nbsp;
interesting if you did please do hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

